1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,442
Research Computing on Explorer and AICR. Scientific Reproducibility. How to make

2
00:00:04,462 --> 00:00:10,122
your computational work redoable, by you in six months or by anyone else, with

3
00:00:10,142 --> 00:00:12,150
environments, data layout and records.

4
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:16,444
Hallo. This is Liam, in for Bear. First, a correction. Reproducibility is not a

5
00:00:16,464 --> 00:00:20,991
favour you do for other scientists. The first person it helps is you, six months

6
00:00:21,011 --> 00:00:25,841
from now, when a reviewer asks for one more plot. This lecture is a toolkit for

7
00:00:25,861 --> 00:00:30,084
making your research redoable: the three words people confuse, why the field has a

8
00:00:30,104 --> 00:00:33,722
crisis, a checklist, how to record your software, conda environments done properly,

9
00:00:33,742 --> 00:00:36,450
data organisation, documentation, and what to do tomorrow morning.

10
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:40,724
Six terms. Version control: software that records every change to your files, so you

11
00:00:40,744 --> 00:00:45,250
can go back. A module: a piece of software the cluster team installed, loaded by

12
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:49,172
name and version. Conda: a tool that builds isolated environments of packages. An

13
00:00:49,192 --> 00:00:53,396
environment: one named set of software, kept apart from all the others. A README:

14
00:00:53,416 --> 00:00:57,921
the plain-text file that explains a folder. And a random seed: the number that makes

15
00:00:57,941 --> 00:00:59,430
a random process repeat exactly.

16
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:03,638
Three words people mix up. Reproducibility: someone else takes your same data and

17
00:01:03,658 --> 00:01:07,796
your same methods and gets your result. Replicability: a different team collects new

18
00:01:07,816 --> 00:01:11,314
data, uses similar methods, and finds a comparable result. Repeatability: you

19
00:01:11,334 --> 00:01:16,112
yourself re-run the same methods on the same data six months later and get the

20
00:01:16,132 --> 00:01:19,630
identical answer. Today is mostly about the first and the third.

21
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:24,920
Why it matters. In 2016 a Nature survey asked scientists whether they had failed to

22
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:29,811
reproduce an experiment. Most had: in chemistry, roughly nine in ten had failed to

23
00:01:29,831 --> 00:01:34,003
reproduce someone else's result; in biology, nearly eight in ten; in physics,

24
00:01:34,023 --> 00:01:39,243
medicine and earth science, around two in three. Other studies put numbers on it. In

25
00:01:39,263 --> 00:01:43,085
psychology, only thirty-six to forty-seven of a hundred studies replicated. In

26
00:01:43,105 --> 00:01:46,579
biomedicine, eleven percent of fifty-three landmark cancer studies. In economics,

27
00:01:46,599 --> 00:01:51,120
only forty-nine percent of papers even had enough data and code to try.

28
00:01:51,190 --> 00:01:55,425
It bites in the real world: clinical decisions built on results no one can

29
00:01:55,445 --> 00:01:59,984
reproduce, policy built on models no one can re-run. And it bites you first. In

30
00:02:00,004 --> 00:02:04,239
every project something has to be redone, an analysis, a plot. The more reproducible

31
00:02:04,259 --> 00:02:08,494
your workflow, the faster that is, and the faster you publish. The usual culprits

32
00:02:08,514 --> 00:02:12,445
are five: methods not documented, data and code not shared, software versions not

33
00:02:12,465 --> 00:02:16,396
recorded, results that depend on one machine, and absolute file paths baked into

34
00:02:16,416 --> 00:02:16,700
code.

35
00:02:16,770 --> 00:02:21,092
The checklist, in three phases. At setup: a Git repository, a consistent folder

36
00:02:21,112 --> 00:02:24,767
layout, a documented environment, a README. During the work: commented code,

37
00:02:24,787 --> 00:02:28,441
relative paths, random seeds set, every data transformation written down, regular

38
00:02:28,461 --> 00:02:33,452
commits. At the end: have someone else run it, ship the environment file and the

39
00:02:33,472 --> 00:02:37,460
instructions, share the data and the code, and write down the limitations.

40
00:02:37,530 --> 00:02:44,483
And one principle to carry: FAIR, set out in Scientific Data in 2016. Findable:

41
00:02:44,503 --> 00:02:48,965
persistent identifiers and metadata. Accessible: stored in open repositories.

42
00:02:48,985 --> 00:02:52,950
Interoperable: standard formats. Reusable: clear licensing and documentation.

43
00:02:53,020 --> 00:02:57,776
Version control gets its own lecture, but the idea in one breath: every change is

44
00:02:57,796 --> 00:03:01,278
recorded, you can revert to any earlier version, collaborate without overwriting

45
00:03:01,298 --> 00:03:05,735
each other, keep the whole history, and experiment on a branch without breaking the

46
00:03:05,755 --> 00:03:06,690
code that works.

47
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:10,877
Record your software. If you load a cluster module, write down the version and when

48
00:03:10,897 --> 00:03:16,117
you ran it, in the job script or a README. A job script like this one is already a

49
00:03:16,137 --> 00:03:20,254
record: Julia one point ten point four, B W A zero point seven point eighteen,

50
00:03:20,274 --> 00:03:24,667
bamtools two point five point two. Change it, and copy the file first, or let Git

51
00:03:24,687 --> 00:03:28,528
keep the versions. The team's own module install scripts are public on GitHub, so

52
00:03:28,548 --> 00:03:31,010
you can see exactly how a module was built.

53
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:36,056
And don't load modules in your dot bash R C. That hidden file runs in every shell

54
00:03:36,076 --> 00:03:40,171
and every program you open on the cluster. Aliases and colours belong there. Modules

55
00:03:40,191 --> 00:03:44,286
don't: they break the self-contained job script, so later you can't tell whether a

56
00:03:44,306 --> 00:03:48,400
program came from the script or the shell, and they cause dependency conflicts. Keep

57
00:03:48,420 --> 00:03:49,870
software in scripts and READMEs.

58
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:54,506
Conda builds isolated environments. It works on every platform, handles Python and

59
00:03:54,526 --> 00:03:57,946
non-Python packages, keeps each project's dependencies apart, and resolves

60
00:03:57,966 --> 00:04:03,297
conflicts. On the cluster: get a compute node, load a miniconda module, create an

61
00:04:03,317 --> 00:04:07,883
environment with a Python version, activate it with source activate, install, and

62
00:04:07,903 --> 00:04:09,030
deactivate when done.

63
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:13,331
Seven habits. Pin the versions that matter; unpinned, conda takes the latest. Prefer

64
00:04:13,351 --> 00:04:16,929
the conda-forge channel, the best maintained. Channel order matters: conda searches

65
00:04:16,949 --> 00:04:21,180
top to bottom. Install conda packages first, then pip ones. Export the environment,

66
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:25,431
with from history for a cleaner file. Install everything in one session, because

67
00:04:25,451 --> 00:04:30,010
conda resolves conflicts across the whole set at once, and an environment grown over

68
00:04:30,030 --> 00:04:34,588
months is the one that won't rebuild. And keep environments small: one per analysis,

69
00:04:34,608 --> 00:04:36,550
not one to rule them all.

70
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:41,539
Picture the two ways to build one. Added to over months, package by package, the

71
00:04:41,559 --> 00:04:45,820
environment collects conflicts, and when you try to recreate it, it won't resolve.

72
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:50,100
Built in one session from one file, it resolves once and rebuilds anywhere.

73
00:04:50,170 --> 00:04:53,850
Managing them. List your environments. Create one at a specific path, in your

74
00:04:53,870 --> 00:04:58,688
project space. Remove one. Clone one as a backup. Update one from its Y M L file,

75
00:04:58,708 --> 00:05:03,241
pruning what the file no longer lists. If solving is slow, install mamba and use it

76
00:05:03,261 --> 00:05:07,225
in place of conda. And always use source activate: conda activate needs conda init,

77
00:05:07,245 --> 00:05:10,640
which rewrites your dot bash R C and fights with Open OnDemand.

78
00:05:10,710 --> 00:05:15,805
The complete workflow starts from a file. One environment dot Y M L names the

79
00:05:15,825 --> 00:05:20,238
environment, the channels in order, the pinned Python and packages, and the pip

80
00:05:20,258 --> 00:05:20,920
extras last.

81
00:05:20,990 --> 00:05:25,566
Make the project folder, start Git, create the environment from the file, activate

82
00:05:25,586 --> 00:05:30,868
it, work, export the final environment, and commit both files. In a job script the

83
00:05:30,888 --> 00:05:35,110
same two lines, module load and source activate, make the run reproducible.

84
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:39,313
Data. Three folders: raw, which is original and read-only; processed, cleaned and

85
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:43,466
transformed; and metadata, dictionaries and sample IDs. Each with its own README.

86
00:05:43,486 --> 00:05:48,310
Make raw read-only with c h mod five five five, with your professor's agreement,

87
00:05:48,330 --> 00:05:53,155
since only the owner can change permissions. And name files to sort themselves: ISO

88
00:05:53,175 --> 00:05:56,270
dates, version numbers, lowercase, underscores or hyphens, never spaces.

89
00:05:56,340 --> 00:06:00,795
Every data folder needs a README: where the data came from, what each variable means

90
00:06:00,815 --> 00:06:05,270
and its range, what processing was done, and what's known to be wrong with it.

91
00:06:05,340 --> 00:06:09,457
A data dictionary makes the variables a table: name, type, description, units,

92
00:06:09,477 --> 00:06:14,628
range, and the missing code. And back it all up by the three-two-one rule: three

93
00:06:14,648 --> 00:06:17,730
copies, on two different media, one of them off-site.

94
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:22,980
The project README: what it is in two sentences, how to install, how to run, where

95
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,530
the data is, and the reproduction steps as a numbered list someone can follow

96
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:29,480
without you. Then citation, licence, contact.

97
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:33,816
In code, give functions docstrings: what it does, parameters, what it returns. And

98
00:06:33,836 --> 00:06:38,103
comment the why, not the what. 'Adjust for one-based indexing in the original

99
00:06:38,123 --> 00:06:41,730
dataset' tells a reader something. 'Add one to x' does not.

100
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:46,635
Five things to keep, and the exercise. Use version control from day one. Document

101
00:06:46,655 --> 00:06:50,449
your environment with conda or pip. Organise files systematically: data, source,

102
00:06:50,469 --> 00:06:53,570
results. Write the documentation: README, comments, dictionaries. And test

103
00:06:53,590 --> 00:06:57,732
reproducibility by having someone else run your code. The exercise: write an

104
00:06:57,752 --> 00:07:02,240
environment dot yml with Python three point ten, numpy, pandas and matplotlib; build

105
00:07:02,260 --> 00:07:07,095
it; activate it; install a couple more packages; export the final file. Start the

106
00:07:07,115 --> 00:07:11,949
next project this way, document as you go, and share when you can. Perfect

107
00:07:11,969 --> 00:07:14,030
reproducibility is hard; any improvement counts.

108
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:18,229
Let's recap with Claude. Reproducible means same data, same methods, and someone

109
00:07:18,249 --> 00:07:23,070
else gets your result. Most scientists have failed to reproduce a result, and the

110
00:07:23,090 --> 00:07:28,257
first person you help is yourself. At setup: Git, a layout, an environment file, a

111
00:07:28,277 --> 00:07:32,060
README; during: relative paths, seeds, commits. Job scripts record module versions,

112
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:36,901
and modules never go in dot bash R C. Conda: pin versions, prefer conda-forge,

113
00:07:36,921 --> 00:07:41,051
install in one session, keep environments small, export them, and use source

114
00:07:41,071 --> 00:07:44,163
activate. Data: raw read-only, READMEs, dictionaries, three-two-one backups. And

115
00:07:44,183 --> 00:07:45,200
comment the why.

116
00:07:45,270 --> 00:07:49,631
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Here is how my current research project is

117
00:07:49,651 --> 00:07:53,698
organised and what software it uses: [describe it in three sentences]. Audit it

118
00:07:53,718 --> 00:07:56,514
against the reproducibility checklist: version control, environment file, folder

119
00:07:56,534 --> 00:07:59,956
layout, README, relative paths, random seeds, data README and dictionary, backups.

120
00:07:59,976 --> 00:08:05,588
List what is missing in order of how much it would hurt me six months from now, and

121
00:08:05,608 --> 00:08:09,656
write the environment.yml and the README skeleton for me. Then check two things

122
00:08:09,676 --> 00:08:14,976
yourself. Did it pin versions in the Y M L? And could a stranger follow the README's

123
00:08:14,996 --> 00:08:16,540
steps without asking you anything?

124
00:08:16,610 --> 00:08:21,150
Scientific Reproducibility. At Nik Bear Brown.
